A||||6||BlackARROW||Red Bomb on the Duke - Backpressure?||||5||
Z||000000||BlackARROW||09-05-2001||11:39 AM||||FINALLY!!  After many months, many analysis, I have fixed my "crazy kindda Turbo sound" that was causing my a loss of power over 3000 rpm (2nd gear and on).  <P>Before looking for my muffler, which was the target prior to look for the manifold. (since it was definitely coming from the muffler region (tests done with a mic on the highway - pretty fun) I started by cutting the end of the pipe: changing the standard "split in two"-end by a nice chrome straight end (no split).  Thinking, I'll go from the end to the beginning.  Believe it or not, my "high pitch" sound has disappeared.  Maybe, after all, it was doing like a whistle - kind of - but very powerful sound.  Maybe that's why I never saw deers on the roads    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Now I seem to have no power loss over 3000 - but anyway, who said the Duke was powerful    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Question?   Since I wanted to change my muffler (thinking it could whistle in there), I was thinking of changing it for a Cherry Bomb (straight - less backpressure - suppozadly more performant?! ya right    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.  My concern is the backpressure.  I know the motor needs it, but since I have removed my catalyser, now my end pipe, would it be to much to un-restrict the muffler too?<P>Since the muffler look like this:<BR>¯¯¯¯__<BR>  _|­¯[__]_     (a bit twisted - a bit restrictive)<P>Was thinking of changing it for this:<BR>  _&lt;====&gt;_    (yup - &lt;====&gt; is the Cherry Bomb :P)  24" seems to fit if I just cut before the corners.<P>Thanks to you<P>Black-&gt;<BR>Fiero 2m4 '85<P><BR>[This message has been edited by BlackARROW (edited 09-05-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by BlackARROW (edited 09-05-2001).]||64.229.203.47||reg||5
Z||000001||OcalaFiero||09-05-2001||11:53 AM||||I did almost the same thing this weekend. I had to replace my Cat, so I just removed it and put a Cherry Bomb Turbo II muffler (looks like a regualr muffler not like a normal cherry bomb) in place of the cat and used flex pipe to reach where the cat connected to the exhaust pie and the cut the main pipe to put a tailpipe on before the point where the old muffler was located. The back pressure doesn't seem to be an issue and it sounds better.||||reg||1
Z||000002||artherd||09-05-2001||06:02 PM||||Congratulations, you just lost a few hp  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by OcalaFiero:<BR><B>I did almost the same thing this weekend. I had to replace my Cat, so I just removed it and put a Cherry Bomb Turbo II muffler (looks like a regualr muffler not like a normal cherry bomb) in place of the cat and used flex pipe to reach where the cat connected to the exhaust pie and the cut the main pipe to put a tailpipe on before the point where the old muffler was located. The back pressure doesn't seem to be an issue and it sounds better.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000003||Simple Fiero||09-05-2001||06:35 PM||||So why has he lost HP!, I am just curious, trying to understand all this stuff.<BR>also I was thinking of doing a dual muffler system, 1: am I stupid?<BR>2: Will it work?<BR>3: What will happen to my car if i do?<BR>4: am I stupid?||||reg||5
Z||000004||sluppy123||09-05-2001||06:53 PM||||"Congratulations, you just lost a few hp"<P>That's kind of a smart A$$ answer, don't ya think?<P>What he means is you lost all the back pressure when you took out the muffler. Back pressure helps make low-end torque. <P>------------------<BR>-Brian, Toolmaker<BR>Lincoln, NE<BR>87' GT-Galaxy silver mettalic, Turbo Charged, Modified 2.8- 235 HP w/ 7 psi Boost, Eibach Sport springs, RSM side scoops, Mr Mikes leather, 17" Mille Miglia's. <BR>               <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1711832&a=12781563&p=48213929&Sequence=0"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||maryjane||09-05-2001||10:02 PM||||Ok Artherd or Sluppy, I've always heard ya need some backpress for proper eng performance. How does back pressure help and as a general rule, how much is too much. I'm not doubting you at all, just ignorant of how this works. Keep it simple please, my brain is old. Thanks<BR>Don||||reg||1
Z||000006||ocalafiero1||09-05-2001||10:10 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B>Congratulations, you just lost a few hp    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>It wasn't done to increase performance at all. The 2.5 in my '86 has some "issues" and had a melted down cat, so much so that it nearly blew itself off of the car. I agree, there is a loss of torque that can be felt, but it has solved the problems (at least temporarily) that I was having with back pressure. BTW it was a TurboII muffler, not the "classic" Cherry Bomb style tube muffler (similar in theory to a FlowMaster). Once I get to the point where I can rebuild or upgrade to another engine (baby on the way and payments on our "real" cars). Removing the cat or putting a tube muffler are definately not  "mod's" that should be done to increase horsepower.<p>[This message has been edited by ocalafiero1 (edited 09-05-2001).]||||reg||1
